On 2008-03-17, ghost@256k.org <ghost@256k.org> wrote:
> The barbarian is probably the least interesting class in the game as
> it stands right now. 

For future reference: the Spork barbarian is likely the easiest role in
Spork right now.  The two-hander changes make him immensely strong in
melee, the HP gain changes affect him least of all roles due to his
natural HP and starting CON, and the available artifacts cater to him in
a number of convenient ways.

All this adds up to something I quite approve of; a good 'starter' class
for people that isn't terribly complicated (spells are likely a lost 
cause for this guy without a ton of work) and has a lot of durability.
He's in good shape right now -- perhaps he is the wrong target?

> It sits squarely in the middle of the original
> undifferentiated combat wombat class group and is little more than a
> halfway point between the valkyrie and the caveman (and while those
> two are more different from each other than they are in vanilla,
> they're still pretty similar).

Does the Barbarian need changing here, or do the Valk and Caveman?
(the Caveman does, actually, have quite a few changes; the Valk is
probably the visible problem here, being not much more than a slightly
flimsier Knight with cold resistance.)

> STARTING INVENTORY:
> EITHER (two handed sword and axe) OR (battle axe and short sword)
> 2-4 uncursed potions of speed
> 0-3 uncursed potions of booze
> a horn (70% tooled, 10% fire, 10% frost, 10% plenty)

While startscumming isn't productive in and of itself (if you can't
ascend without startscumming, you likely can't ascend _with_
startscumming), I still don't want to encourage it.

Thus, 2 potions of speed and a tooled horn; no randomness.  (Potions of
booze are only nominally useful in the early game, and while it'd be
interesting to have booze affect your combat stats in a traditional
Fallout-like manner, the game doesn't track drunk yet; it's no big deal
to have those be random.)

> That's it. No food and no armor. The lack of food is an encouragement
> to get killing things right away. More on the armor issue follows.

Does any role need encouragement to start doing that?  After all,
everything in the dungeon is out to get you, for the most part, and even
the weaker melee classes are delighted to let Fido mop up for them.

> AC BONUS:

(snipped 'AC bonus for wearing no armor in a slot'; more later, but
consider that gloves are nearly mandatory in the dungeon after a certain
point due to blindness/footrice corpse issues, not to mention being able
to pick up the footrice corpses so that others cannot use them)

> RECKLESSNESS BONUS:
> XP1: Magic Cancellation 1
> XP4: Magic Cancellation 2
> XP8: Magic Cancellation 3
> XP12: Magic Resistance

No role in Spork will ever gain intrinsic magic resistance; in fact, I'd
like to see it become less common on the Quest Artifacts themselves, and
possibly more common in some other overloaded slots.  But the intrinsic
leads down a path I will not travel.

I'm also not happy with the "magic cancellation" intrinsic, but that's
at least partly due to the wide range of things that MC affects; the
whole concept of MC and what it's supposed to represent needs a re-look
before something like this can be considered.

> As a quick case study: assuming no divine protection, a level fifteen
> barbarian wearing +4 SDSM and a +4 Cloak of Displacement would have an
> AC of -16 and a damage bonus of +5. If they put on a full standard
> Ascension Kit, they would have in the area of -27 AC in addition to
> the other effects of the magic armor, but no Damage bonus and a lot of
> additional weight.

...which means that people will simply put on the ascension kit and have
done with it, because AC -16 isn't enough in Spork.  Weight isn't an issue 
past the midgame.

> NEGATIVES:
> The monster generation soft clock increases at a significantly faster
> rate for barbarians, reaching its invocation level maximum as early as
> turn 20,000.

This is too fast; the Barbarian as described will not be able to handle
the max-rate.  (I don't think you've played since I made the
monster-clock changes, so you may wish to poke around a bit more before
considering something like this -- specifically, you should try an
ascension run from the Sanctum on up, to see what "max clock" really
looks like.)

> Barbarians tend to encounter difficult monsters somewhat sooner. The
> monster generation formula for barbarians is [difficulty <=
> ((xplvl*1.25) + dlvl)/2] instead of [difficulty <= (xplvl+dlvl)/2]

Since the above is too fast anyway, this is even worse.

> Rodney related effects happen slighly more often to barbarians after
> the invocation.

That increases the potential for cursed two-handers and "summon
nasties".  The ascension run is already becoming a very hairy place to
be in Spork; there's no need to outright try to kill people.

> OTHER MINOR IDEAS:
> Barbarians get a small Damage bonus when confused.

Drunk would be more appropriate, really, but that will have to wait 'til
drunkenness as a state is implemented.  But I like the idea of _that_.

> New artifact: Sword of the Phoenix (from Conan) 

There will be no new weapon artifacts unless there is a very, very, very
good reason.  There are already too many weapon artifacts in the game.

> I'm curious to know what everyone else on RGRN thinks of these
> ideas and what ideas other people might have about how the Barbarian
> could be more interesting.

I like the idea of the Barbarian getting bonuses for not wearing armor,
but there is a major problem with that: so many magical armors grant
something useful that unless the Barbarian's bonuses make him
significantly _better_ than a standard Ascension Kit, people simply
aren't going to bother and are going to stick with the routine.

Also, my mental picture of "barbarian" includes a raggedy cloak, viking 
helmet, big furry boots, and torn gloves -- so it actually isn't necessarily 
appropriate to deny him any of those items.  I think you may have confused 
"barbarian" with "berserker" (known for rushing into battle naked).

So it might be appropriate to give a barb stiff penalties for wearing
non-leather body armor, t-shirts, or shields, and perhaps give him
something like "berserk while drunk" as well.  To offset the decrease in
AC and inability to benefit from DSM, barbarians could have a chance to 
evade certain types of attacks (as long as not wearing non-leather body 
armor, t-shirts, or shields) that would increase with their Dex and level.

